WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.722
Dates become much more useful after they are converted to a real datetime type.

00:00:04.722 --> 00:00:11.292
In this example, the order_date column is already converted using pd.to_datetime.

00:00:11.292 --> 00:00:13.757
Then we use the dt accessor.

00:00:13.757 --> 00:00:17.107
Dt.month_name returns the month name.

00:00:17.107 --> 00:00:20.327
Dt.day_name returns the weekday. Dt.quarter returns the quarter number.

00:00:20.327 --> 00:00:23.552
Click Run and inspect the new columns.

00:00:23.552 --> 00:00:26.682
These features can support many analytical questions.

00:00:26.682 --> 00:00:29.957
Do sales change by weekday?

00:00:29.957 --> 00:00:34.272
Are there seasonal patterns? Which quarter had the most transactions?

00:00:34.272 --> 00:00:40.357
Are hospital admissions higher during certain months?

00:00:40.357 --> 00:00:44.942
We can also calculate differences between dates, extract the year, or group records by time periods.

00:00:44.942 --> 00:00:51.237
The key lesson is that a date stored as text is limited.

00:00:51.237 --> 00:00:55.927
A true datetime value gives us access to time-based operations.

00:00:55.927 --> 00:01:00.777
Before using a date in analysis, confirm that it was parsed successfully and check for invalid values that

00:01:00.777 --> 00:01:03.500
became missing.
